<ContentLayout title="About StackGeist" description="How StackGeist approaches setup inspiration, gear recommendations, and affiliate links.">
  <p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">StackGeist</a> / About</p>
  <p class="page-kicker">About StackGeist</p>
  <h1 class="page-title">Useful setup ideas first. Affiliate links second.</h1>
  <p class="page-lead">StackGeist is built and edited by Keith Casko. It focuses on practical troubleshooting, desk setups, homelab and PC-adjacent gear, and buying decisions that solve a specific problem.</p>

  <section class="content-section">
    <div class="cards"><div class="card"><h2>Who writes StackGeist</h2><p><strong>Keith Casko</strong> writes and reviews the site’s troubleshooting and gear research. The work draws on hands-on PC, Windows, Linux, homelab, networking, cloud, and desk-setup experience. Pages distinguish personal use from source-backed research instead of pretending every product was tested.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h2>How recommendations work</h2><p>We start with the setup problem, then look for products that fit the space, use case, and constraints. Products migrated from the TaurusTech affiliate catalog were personally purchased and used by Keith; other pages are labeled as researched.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h2>Affiliate disclosure</h2><p>Some retailer links are affiliate links. As an Amazon Associate I earn from qualifying purchases. A commission does not change the price you pay.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h2>What we verify</h2><p>Ownership records practical familiarity, not a laboratory test. Technical specifications and compatibility claims remain tied to identified sources. Price and stock can change after publication.</p><p><a href="/editorial-methodology">Read the editorial methodology →</a></p></div>
      <div class="card"><h2>What we avoid</h2><p>No fake urgency, fake reviews, fabricated experience, or lists of products added only because they have an affiliate link.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>
</ContentLayout>
